= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Fix a <Young tableau> $t$ of shape $\lambda$, let $C(t)$ be its column stabilizer, and put
$$
b_t=\sum_{g\in C(t)}\operatorname{sgn}(g)g,\qquad e_t=b_t\{t\}.
$$
These are the <column antisymmetrizer of a Young tableau> and its <polytabloid>. The <Specht module> is generated by $e_t$ as a <cyclic module>, so the nonzero <R-module homomorphism> $\phi$ has $\phi(e_t)\ne0$. Because its extension $\Phi$ commutes with the <group algebra> action,
$$
0\ne\phi(e_t)=\Phi(b_t\{t\})=b_t\Phi(\{t\}).
$$
Expand $\Phi(\{t\})$ in the <tabloid> basis of the <Young permutation module> $M^\mu$. At least one <tabloid> $\{u\}$ therefore satisfies $b_t\{u\}\ne0$.

If a row of $u$ contains two entries from the same column of $t$, their transposition $\tau\in C(t)$ fixes $\{u\}$. Pairing the terms indexed by $g$ and $g\tau$ in $b_t\{u\}$ makes them cancel. This remains valid in <characteristic> two, since the paired coefficients sum to zero there as well. Thus each row of $u$ meets each column of $t$ in at most one entry.

The first $r$ rows of $u$ consequently contain at most $\min(r,\lambda'_j)$ entries from column $j$ of $t$, where $\lambda'$ is the <conjugate partition>. Hence
$$
\sum_{i=1}^r\mu_i\leq\sum_j\min(r,\lambda'_j)=\sum_{i=1}^r\lambda_i
\qquad(r\geq1).
$$
By the <dominance order on partitions>, this proves
$$
\boxed{\lambda\unrhd\mu.}
$$
This is <dominance from a nonzero column antisymmetrizer>. The extension is what allows us to apply $b_t$ to an element of $M^\mu$ without dividing by the column stabilizer's order, which might vanish in the given <field>.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Young permutation module> $M^{(n-k,k)}$ is the <symmetric-group subset permutation representation> on the $k$-element subsets of $\{1,\ldots,n\}$: the second row of a <tabloid> specifies the subset. For $k\geq1$, <restriction of a character> to $S_{n-1}$ fixing $n$ splits these subsets into two <orbits of a group action>, according as they contain $n$.

The corresponding <permutation representations> are on the $k$-subsets and the $(k-1)$-subsets of $\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$. Their <Young permutation modules> have two row sizes $n-1-k,k$ and $n-k,k-1$, respectively. Sort each pair into decreasing order and omit any zero part; swapping the two row labels gives an isomorphic <permutation representation>. This sorting matters at $n=2k$.

We use <Young's rule>: over the <complex numbers>, the multiplicity of $S^\rho$ in $M^\eta$ is the <Kostka number> $K_{\rho\eta}$, and it can be nonzero only if $\rho$ dominates $\eta$ in the <dominance order on partitions>. Since each $\eta$ above has at most two parts and size $n-1$, dominance gives
$$
\rho_1+\rho_2\geq\eta_1+\eta_2=n-1.
$$
Thus $\rho$ has at most two nonzero parts. The <character inner product> measures this constituent multiplicity, so
$$
\boxed{\left\langle\operatorname{Res}_{S_{n-1}}^{S_n}\pi^{(n-k,k)},\chi^\rho\right\rangle=0\quad\text{when }\ell(\rho)\geq3.}
$$
If $k=0$, the original and restricted modules are the <trivial representations>, giving the same conclusion. For $n=1$, there is no partition of $n-1=0$ with three parts, so the assertion is vacuous.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Apply <Young's rule>, stated as
$$
M^{(n-k,k)}\cong\bigoplus_{\rho\vdash n}(S^\rho)^{\oplus K_{\rho,(n-k,k)}}.
$$
Here the <Kostka number> counts <semistandard Young tableaux> of shape $\rho$ with $n-k$ entries equal to $1$ and $k$ entries equal to $2$. Their rows weakly increase and their columns strictly increase. A column can therefore have at most two cells, so only a <partition of an integer> $\rho=(n-j,j)$ can occur.

In such a <semistandard Young tableau>, all $j$ entries in the second row must be $2$ and all $j$ entries above them must be $1$. The whole first row is then fixed by the content: its first $n-k$ entries are $1$ and its remaining $k-j$ entries are $2$. This is possible exactly when $j\leq k$ and $j\leq n-k$. Because $k\leq n/2$, the second inequality follows from the first. There is exactly one filling for every $0\leq j\leq k$, and none for any other shape.

Consequently each displayed <Specht module> has multiplicity one, proving the <two-row Young permutation module decomposition>
$$
\boxed{M^{(n-k,k)}\cong\bigoplus_{j=0}^k S^{(n-j,j)}.}
$$
For $j=0$, the zero second part is omitted. As a dimension check, the <Hook-length formula> gives $\dim S^{(n-j,j)}=\binom nj-\binom n{j-1}$, with $\binom n{-1}=0$; the sum telescopes to $\binom nk$, the dimension of the original <permutation representation>.

