= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <hook criterion in a beta set>: for a finite <beta set of a partition> $X$, each <hook length> is a difference $b-c$ with $b\in X$, $0\leq c<b$ and $c\notin X$, and every such difference is a hook length.

A hook length $pr$ therefore gives a bead $b$ and a gap $c=b-pr$. Along the progression
$$
b,\ b-p,\ b-2p,\ldots,\ b-rp=c,
$$
the first position is a bead and the last is a gap. Some adjacent positions are consequently a bead followed by a gap. Their difference is $p$, so $p$ is a <hook length>. Similarly the progression from $b$ to $c$ in steps of $r$ gives a hook length $r$. Every position used is nonnegative because it lies between $c$ and $b$.

Thus the <divisor closure of hook lengths> yields
$$
\boxed{pr\in\mathcal H(\lambda)\ \Longrightarrow\ p,r\in\mathcal H(\lambda).}
$$
Neither $p$ nor $r$ needs to be a <prime number>.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The converse is **false**. Take $p=2$, $r=3$, $n=6$ and the <partition of an integer> $\lambda=(3,3)$. Its <hook lengths>, arranged in its <Young diagram>, are
$$
\begin{matrix}
4&3&2\\
3&2&1
\end{matrix}.
$$
Both $2$ and $3$ occur, but $6$ does not, even though $pr=6=n$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\lambda=(3,3),\quad p=2,\quad r=3}
$$
is a counterexample with distinct $p,r$.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $L=h_{i,j}=pr$. The <hook-interval decomposition at a Young-diagram cell> is the disjoint union
$$
\{1,\ldots,L\}
=\{h_{i,y}:j\leq y\leq\lambda_i\}
\sqcup\{L-h_{x,j}:i<x\leq\lambda'_j\}.
$$
Here is a proof of the identity using the <hook criterion in a beta set>. Keep only the cells weakly southeast of $(i,j)$, translating that cell to $(1,1)$; this gives the <partition of an integer>
$$
\nu=(\lambda_i-j+1,\ldots,\lambda_{\lambda'_j}-j+1).
$$
Let $\ell=\lambda'_j-i+1$ and use its <beta set of a partition> $b_s=\nu_s+\ell-s$. These numbers are precisely the <hook lengths> down the original column from $(i,j)$, with $b_1=L$. The hooks along the first row of $\nu$ are the differences $L-c$ for the gaps $0\leq c<L$. The remaining positions in that interval are the beads $b_2,\ldots,b_\ell$, giving exactly the displayed disjoint decomposition. The first-row and first-column <hook lengths> of $\nu$ agree with those in the original <Young-diagram hook>.

Because $p\mid L$, a leg cell satisfies $p\mid h_{x,j}$ exactly when $p\mid L-h_{x,j}$. Thus divisibility by $p$ identifies the relevant cells of the <Young-diagram hook> with the multiples of $p$ in $\{1,\ldots,pr\}$. There are exactly $r$ of those, proving
$$
\boxed{\left|\{(x,y)\in H_{(i,j)}(\lambda):p\mid h_{x,y}(\lambda)\}\right|=r.}
$$
The count includes the corner cell $(i,j)$ itself.

= 3
{parent=Paper 103}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The equivalence is **true**. Write $c_r(\eta)$ for the total size at level $r$ of the <core tower of a partition> $\eta$, and $q_r(\eta)$ for the corresponding total size in its <quotient tower of a partition>. We use the size relation and the <P-adic valuation of a symmetric-group character degree from the core tower>:
$$
|\eta|=\sum_{r\geq0}c_r(\eta)p^r,
\qquad
\nu_p(\chi^\eta(1))=\frac{\sum_{r\geq0}c_r(\eta)-d_p(|\eta|)}{p-1}.
$$
Here $d_p$ is the <base-p digit sum>. These imply the <character degree coprime to p from the core tower> criterion: if $|\eta|=\sum_r\alpha_rp^r$, then $p\nmid\chi^\eta(1)$ exactly when $c_r(\eta)=\alpha_r$ at every level. Indeed carrying $p$ units from one position to the next decreases the sum of coefficients by $p-1$; the valuation vanishes precisely when there are no carries.

For $k\geq1$, we also use <power-core truncation of a prime-core tower>: for $\gamma=C_{p^k}(\lambda)$, its core tower agrees with that of $\lambda$ below level $k$ and is empty at every level at least $k$. Moreover $q_k(\lambda)=w_{p^k}(\lambda)$, the <weight of a partition> for modulus $p^k$. To see the truncation, removal of a <rim hook> of length $p^k$ preserves the first $p$-core and, under the <abacus divisible-hook correspondence>, becomes removal of a $p^{k-1}$-hook in one quotient component. Iterating preserves all the lower cores; after all such removals, quotient level $k$ is empty.

If $p\nmid\chi^\lambda(1)$, the criterion gives $c_k(\lambda)=a$, $c_r(\lambda)=0$ for $r>k$, and the lower $c_r(\lambda)$ are the base-$p$ digits of $m$. It follows that $|\gamma|=m$, $p\nmid\chi^\gamma(1)$ and
$$
w_{p^k}(\lambda)=\frac{n-|\gamma|}{p^k}=a.
$$
Conversely, suppose $w_{p^k}(\lambda)=a$ and $p\nmid\chi^\gamma(1)$. Then $|\gamma|=m$ and its lower core-tower sizes are the digits of $m$. Quotient level $k$ of $\lambda$ has total size $a<p$. Each component there has size less than $p$, hence is already a $p$-core and has empty $p$-quotient. Thus $c_k(\lambda)=a$ and every higher level is empty. Combined with truncation, this gives exactly the digits of $n$, so $p\nmid\chi^\lambda(1)$.

Therefore
$$
\boxed{p\nmid\chi^\lambda(1)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ w_{p^k}(\lambda)=a\text{ and }p\nmid\chi^{C_{p^k}(\lambda)}(1).}
$$
If $k=0$, necessarily $m=0$ and $n=a<p$; the $1$-core is empty, its weight is $n=a$, and the <Hook-length formula> shows that every degree is coprime to $p$. Thus the equivalence holds in that boundary case too, with the empty partition having degree one.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The implication is **false**. Choose
$$
\boxed{p=3,\quad k=2,\quad a=1,\quad m=4,\quad n=13,\quad\lambda=(12,1).}
$$
The inequalities $0\leq4<9$ and the relation $13=1\cdot9+4$ hold. At cell $(1,4)$, the <hook length> is $12-4+1=9=ap^k$.

For the <partition of an integer> $(12,1)$, the <Hook-length formula> gives
$$
\chi^{(12,1)}(1)=\frac{13!}{13\cdot11!}=12,
$$
which is divisible by $3$. This also agrees with the degree of the <standard representation of the symmetric group> $S_{13}$.

The obstruction is visible in the <core of a partition>: removing the horizontal <rim hook> of length nine leaves $\gamma=(3,1)$, which has no hook length nine and hence is the $9$-core. Its degree is $\chi^{(3,1)}(1)=3$. The large hook ensures the required <weight of a partition> here, but the core degree is not coprime to $p$.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The implication is **false**. Choose
$$
\boxed{p=3,\quad k=1,\quad a=2,\quad m=0,\quad n=6,\quad\lambda=(3,3).}
$$
The <hook lengths> are
$$
\begin{matrix}
4&3&2\\
3&2&1
\end{matrix},
\qquad
\chi^{(3,3)}(1)=\frac{6!}{4\cdot3\cdot2\cdot3\cdot2\cdot1}=5.
$$
Thus $3$ does not divide this <irreducible character degree>, but no hook has length $ap^k=6$.

There is no contradiction with part (i): removing two horizontal <rim hooks> of length three gives the empty <core of a partition>, so $w_3(\lambda)=2=a$ and the core degree is one. Having total <weight of a partition> two does not force a single hook of length twice the modulus.

= 4
{parent=Paper 103}
{scope}

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

We use the <abacus divisible-hook correspondence> in its multiset form: the <hook lengths> of $\lambda$ that are divisible by $p$, divided by $p$, form exactly the disjoint union of the <multisets> of <hook lengths> of the components of its <quotient of a partition> for modulus $p$.

Iterating this correspondence gives, for every $r\geq1$,
$$
\{h/p^r:h\in\mathcal H(\lambda),\ p^r\mid h\}
=\bigsqcup_{\eta\in T^Q(\lambda)_r}\mathcal H(\eta)
$$
as <multisets>. Each <Young diagram> has one hook for every cell, so the <cardinality> of the right-hand side is $|T^Q(\lambda)_r|$, the sum of the component sizes.

For any positive integer $h$, its <P-adic valuation> is the number of positive $r$ for which $p^r\mid h$. Summing this identity over hooks and interchanging the finite sums proves
$$
\begin{aligned}
\nu_p\!\left(\prod_{h\in\mathcal H(\lambda)}h\right)
&=\sum_{h\in\mathcal H(\lambda)}\sum_{r\geq1}\mathbf1_{p^r\mid h}\\
&=\sum_{r\geq1}|T^Q(\lambda)_r|.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\nu_p\!\left(\prod_{h\in\mathcal H(\lambda)}h\right)=\sum_{r\geq1}|T^Q(\lambda)_r|.}
$$
The sums are finite because hook lengths are bounded by $|\lambda|$, and the total size at each level of the <quotient tower of a partition> decreases by at least a factor $p$ until it reaches zero.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

We use the <core-quotient bijection for partitions>: an ordered $p$-tuple $(\nu_0,\ldots,\nu_{p-1})$ and a fixed $p$-core determine a unique <partition of an integer>, with
$$
|\lambda|=|C_p(\lambda)|+p\sum_i|\nu_i|.
$$
The <irreducible characters> in both $B$ and $C$ therefore correspond to the same ordered tuples of total size $w$. Replacing the empty <core of a partition> by $\gamma$ while keeping the <quotient of a partition> fixed gives a <bijection> $\chi^\lambda\mapsto\chi^\mu$ from $B$ to $C$. In particular $|B|=|C|$.

For such a pair, all levels of the <core tower of a partition> above level zero agree. At level zero, their sizes are $0$ and $m$, respectively. Put $D=\sum_{r\geq1}|T^C(\lambda)_r|=\sum_{r\geq1}|T^C(\mu)_r|$. When $0\leq m<p$, the <base-p digit sum> satisfies
$$
d_p(wp)=d_p(w),\qquad d_p(wp+m)=d_p(w)+m.
$$
The <P-adic valuation of a symmetric-group character degree from the core tower> then gives
$$
\nu_p(\chi^\lambda(1))=\frac{D-d_p(w)}{p-1}
=\frac{m+D-[d_p(w)+m]}{p-1}
=\nu_p(\chi^\mu(1)).
$$
Thus the same bijection preserves degree coprimality to $p$ and restricts to a bijection between the indicated sets of <irreducible characters of degree coprime to p>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{|B|=|C|,\qquad
|B\cap\operatorname{Irr}_{p'}(S_{wp})|=|C\cap\operatorname{Irr}_{p'}(S_n)|.}
$$
For $m=0$, take $\gamma$ to be the empty partition; the construction becomes the identity.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For every $\chi^\mu\in C$, level zero of the <core tower of a partition> is its <core of a partition> $\gamma$, of size $m$. The <character degree coprime to p from the core tower> criterion says that if $p\nmid\chi^\mu(1)$, this level must have size equal to the units digit $\alpha_0$ of $n$ in base $p$.

But $0\leq\alpha_0<p$, whereas $m\geq p$. Hence every character in $C$ has degree divisible by $p$, and
$$
\boxed{|C\cap\operatorname{Irr}_{p'}(S_n)|=0.}
$$
Equivalently, the core-tower size coefficient $m$ at position zero needs at least one carry in normalization to the base-$p$ digits. Each carry contributes one to the <P-adic valuation of a symmetric-group character degree from the core tower>.
